  "answer_text": "Opioids are a broad class of drugs that are derived from either natural, semi-synthetic, or fully synthetic sources. At the root of all natural and semi-synthetic opioids is the **opium poppy plant (*Papaver somniferum*)**. \n\nThe three categories of opioids, based on how they are derived, are:\n\n### 1. Natural Opioids (Opiates)\nThese are chemical compounds extracted directly from the natural sap or resin of the opium poppy\u2019s seed pod. Because they are entirely natural, they are historically referred to as **opiates**. \n*   **Examples:** Morphine, codeine, and thebaine.\n\n### 2. Semi-Synthetic Opioids\nThese are created in laboratories by chemically modifying natural opiates extracted from the poppy plant. Scientists take a natural compound (like morphine or thebaine) and alter its chemical structure to make a more potent or differently acting drug.\n*   **Examples:** \n    *   *Heroin* (synthesized from morphine)\n    *   *Oxycodone* (OxyContin, Percocet\u2014synthesized from thebaine)\n    *   *Hydrocodone* (Vicodin\u2014synthesized from codeine or thebaine)\n    *   *Hydromorphone* (Dilaudid)\n\n### 3. Fully Synthetic Opioids\nThese are manufactured entirely in a laboratory using artificial chemical processes. They do not contain any material from the opium poppy plant, but they are chemically engineered to mimic the structure and effects of natural opiates. They bind to the exact same receptors in the brain and can be incredibly potent.\n*   **Examples:** Fentanyl, methadone, tramadol, and pethidine (Demerol).\n\n### How They Work\nRegardless of whether they are harvested from a plant or engineered in a lab, all opioids function the same way: they bind to **opioid receptors** on nerve cells in the brain, spinal cord, and gut. This blocks pain signals from reaching the brain and triggers a release of dopamine, which can produce feelings of intense pain relief and euphoria.",
